Sign in to Anona Compute.

Without flags, anona-compute login starts a device sign-in (RFC 8628): it prints a
URL and a code, opens the browser, and waits while you sign in to the
dashboard, check the code, pick the organization and approve. The CLI then
holds a dashboard session for this machine and trades it for gateway tokens
that last 15 minutes. The session secret is stored in the OS keychain when
one is available, else in credentials.json (mode 0600) next to config.toml;
ANONA_COMPUTE_CREDENTIAL_STORE=file|keyring forces one. anona-compute logout revokes it.

--with-api-key reads an API key (ac_ + 40 lowercase hex characters) from
stdin, saves it to the profile, and checks it against the gateway (an
invalid key is still saved, and the command exits 3; --no-verify skips the
check).

The dashboard is --web-url, else ANONA_COMPUTE_WEB_URL, else the profile's web_url,
else https://app.<domain>.

Usage:
  anona-compute login [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help             help for login
      --no-browser       print the sign-in URL but do not open a browser
      --no-verify        with --with-api-key: save the key without checking it against the gateway
      --org string       organization slug to act in (default: the one picked in the browser)
      --web-url string   dashboard URL (env ANONA_COMPUTE_WEB_URL; default https://app.<domain>)
      --with-api-key     read an API key from stdin instead of signing in through the browser

Global Flags:
      --api-key string             API key (env ANONA_COMPUTE_API_KEY, E2B_API_KEY)
      --api-url string             API URL (env ANONA_COMPUTE_API_URL, E2B_API_URL; default https://api.<E2B_DOMAIN>)
      --json                       JSON output: one object per line on stdout (same as -o json)
  -o, --output string              output format: text or json (default "text")
      --profile string             config profile to use (env ANONA_COMPUTE_PROFILE; default "default")
      --request-timeout duration   timeout for each API request (0 = none)
  -v, --verbose                    verbose output on stderr
